.TH RESTORE-MU2EDAQ-NOTIFY-TUNNEL 1 "August 2026" "mu2edaq-notify 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ"
.SH NAME
restore\-mu2edaq\-notify\-tunnel.sh \- check and rebuild the SSH reverse tunnel to the AWS proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./scripts/restore\-mu2edaq\-notify\-tunnel.sh
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-\-watch\fR]
[\fB\-\-interval\fR \fISEC\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-restarts\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISEC\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Works out whether the reverse tunnel
.RB ( "proxy 127.0.0.1:18095 \-> local 127.0.0.1:8095" )
is actually carrying traffic, and rebuilds it if it is not.
.PP
The tunnel is deliberately unsupervised:
.B ExitOnForwardFailure
and the
.B ServerAlive
keepalives make a broken one die promptly and visibly rather than linger as a
zombie reporting health it does not have. This script is the other half of that
bargain.
.SH HOW IT DECIDES
Health is judged
.IR "end to end" :
the proxy is asked to fetch
.I /api/health
through the tunnel. A listening socket on either end proves nothing about the
path between them, and the interesting failures are exactly the ones where a
socket is present and the path is not.
.PP
Three failure modes, of which only the first is obvious:
.TP
1.
The local
.B ssh
process is gone \(en a network change, a laptop sleep, an OOM kill. The tunnel
log usually ends in
.I "Can't assign requested address"
or
.IR "client_loop: send disconnect" .
.TP
2.
The local process is alive but the remote listener is not: the connection
dropped and neither end has noticed yet. A pidfile check calls this healthy;
the end-to-end check does not.
.TP
3.
The local process is gone but sshd on the proxy still holds
.B 127.0.0.1:18095
from the dead session. This is the one that makes a naive restart fail:
.B ExitOnForwardFailure=yes
means the new tunnel refuses to start while the stale listener is there, and
the error \(en
.I "remote port forwarding failed"
\(en names the symptom rather than the cause. The script clears that listener
before rebuilding.
.PP
Clearing it is done carefully, because PIDs are recycled and the session giving
the order is itself an sshd: the process is killed only if its command line is
.BR sshd ,
and only if it is not the sshd serving this connection. Anything else is
reported and left alone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report what is broken and change nothing. Exits 1 when the tunnel is not
carrying traffic, so it works as a monitoring probe.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Rebuild even when the tunnel is healthy.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Keep checking and restore whenever it breaks. Suitable for launchd; see
EXAMPLES.
.TP
.B \-\-interval \fISEC\fR
Seconds between checks under
.BR \-\-watch .
Default 30.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-restarts \fIN\fR
Give up after
.I N
restores in one
.B \-\-watch
run, so a persistent fault becomes one loud failure rather than a restart loop.
Default 20;
.B 0
removes the limit.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fISEC\fR
Per-ssh-command timeout. Default 8.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
It does not start the EC2 instance \(en that is
.IR scripts/start\-mu2edaq\-notify\-chain.sh .
If the proxy has no reachable address the script says so and stops.
.PP
It does not start the local notification server either, but it checks: a tunnel
rebuilt over a dead server carries nothing but 502s, so that is called out
rather than left to look like success.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_INSTANCE_ID,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_DNS_NAME,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_HOST,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_USER,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_KEY,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_REMOTE_BIND,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_LOCAL_TARGET,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_PIDFILE,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_PROXY_LOGFILE,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_TUNNEL_INTERVAL,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_TUNNEL_MAX_RESTARTS,
.B MU2EDAQ_NOTIFY_TUNNEL_SSH_TIMEOUT.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The tunnel is carrying traffic.
.TP
.B 1
It could not be restored, or
.B \-\-check
found it broken.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Restore it after the endpoint starts returning 502:
.PP
.RS
.nf
scripts/restore\-mu2edaq\-notify\-tunnel.sh
curl \-fsS https://notify.andrewnorman.org/api/health
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use it as a probe without changing anything:
.PP
.RS
.nf
scripts/restore\-mu2edaq\-notify\-tunnel.sh \-\-check || echo "tunnel is down"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep it up for a shift, checking every minute:
.PP
.RS
.nf
scripts/restore\-mu2edaq\-notify\-tunnel.sh \-\-watch \-\-interval 60
.fi
.RE
.PP
Under launchd, so it survives logout \(en put this in
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.mu2edaq.notify-tunnel-watch.plist
with
.B RunAtLoad
and
.B KeepAlive
true, then
.BR "launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id \-u)" :
.PP
.RS
.nf
<array>
  <string>/bin/bash</string>
  <string>\-lc</string>
  <string>cd /path/to/mu2edaq\-phone\-notification\-system &amp;&amp;
    scripts/restore\-mu2edaq\-notify\-tunnel.sh \-\-watch \-\-interval 60</string>
</array>
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2edaq-notify-server (1),
.BR update-notify-dns.sh (1),
.IR scripts/start\-mu2edaq\-notify\-chain.sh ,
.IR docs/reverse\-proxy\-operations.md
